## 4. Abundance feature in the cold r-process

The abundance differences between the hot and cold r-processes become further small when plotted as functions of atomic number. Figure 3 compares the mass-averaged abundances with the solar r-process curve and the measured elements in the r-process-rich Galactic halo star CS 31082-001. All cases, except for T_{\\rm 9f}=1.3, are in reasonable agreement with the solar r-process and measured patterns. This may support the robustness of the abundance curves found in the r-process-rich stars. Note that the total mass of the r-process nuclei (Z\\geq 90) is the same for all cases (\\approx 2.9\\times 10^{-4}\\,M_{\\odot}), since the seed abundances (A\\sim 100) are already produced at T_{9}\\sim 2.5 (>T_{\\rm 9f}). The mass fractions of some measurable elements (and {}^{195}Pt) are presented in Table 1, which also shows a small variation between the hot and cold r-processes (up to a factor of two, except for T_{\\rm 9f}=1.3). These differences are small enough, when considering the uncertainties in the nuclear data as well as in the outflow conditions.

We can see, however, a distinctive feature in the abundance curves around A=200-230 (before \\alpha-decay; _black lines_ in Fig. 1) between the hot and cold r-processes. This is due to their different freezeout histories (Fig. 2e). The hot r-process (_red line_) follows a low S_{\\rm a} (\\approx 2 MeV) path at n-exhaustion. At this S_{\\rm a} path, a trough at A\\approx 200 appears in the abundance curve (before \\alpha-decay, Fig. 1b). This is a consequence that the nuclei at N\\approx 130-140 are predicted to be unstable against neutron capture (see the iso-S_{2n}/2 curves in Fig. 6, Wanajo et al. 2004). On the other hand, the cold r-process (_blue line_) takes a high S_{\\rm a} (\\approx 3 MeV) path at n-exhaustion (Fig. 2e). Therefore, the trough shifts to A\\approx 210. The trough is further eroded by neutron capture without push-back by photodisintegration. As a consequence, the parent nuclei of Pb (A=210-231 and 234; Fig. 1e) result in being significantly deficient. This may lead to a visible difference in the Pb production (Fig. 3 and Table 1), despite the similar Th+U values2.

